WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.901
The judge is a classifier, so measure it like one.

2
00:00:03.181 --> 00:00:04.867
Lesson 5 built a judge.

3
00:00:05.147 --> 00:00:11.760
Now the question is how far to trust it, and the answer is a measurement, not an impression from reading a few critiques.

4
00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:16.861
A binary judge is a classifier: for each output it predicts pass or fail.

5
00:00:17.141 --> 00:00:20.853
The truth it’s trying to predict is what your domain expert would say.

6
00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:30.328
So you collect the expert’s labels on a sample of outputs, run the judge on the same sample, and compare, exactly as you would evaluate any classifier.

7
00:00:30.608 --> 00:00:37.072
Hamel Husain’s guide to LLM judges frames the work this way and is specific about the inputs.

8
00:00:37.352 --> 00:00:49.683
Pick one person (perhaps two) whose judgment defines quality for this product, the principal domain expert, rather than averaging a crowd or letting the engineer who wrote the prompt grade their own work.

9
00:00:49.963 --> 00:00:54.891
Have them label each example pass or fail with a short critique explaining why.

10
00:00:55.171 --> 00:01:04.685
The critiques do double duty: they show the judge’s author what the expert is actually looking for, and the clearest ones become few-shot examples in the judge prompt.

11
00:01:04.965 --> 00:01:18.064
Anthropic’s agent-evals write-up puts the goal plainly: calibrate model graders closely against human experts until their judgments rarely diverge, and then a person only needs to spot-check occasionally.

12
00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:35.901
Shankar and colleagues (2024), who built a tool for aligning LLM graders with human grades, add a warning from their user study: alignment is iterative and somewhat subjective, because people refine their criteria as they grade (criteria drift).

13
00:01:36.181 --> 00:01:39.424
Budget for the expert to change a few labels as they go.

14
00:01:39.704 --> 00:01:44.248
If they do, apply the change everywhere, and do it before you look at the test split.
